 {"topic": "Tracheostomy Emergency",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-tracheostomy-emergency",
  "stem": "A 68-year-old man who had a total laryngectomy 2 years ago is brought to the emergency department in respiratory distress with oxygen saturation of 80%. Paramedics have been applying a nonrebreather mask over his nose and mouth for 15 minutes without improvement. He has a permanent stoma in the lower neck with no tube in place, and thick crusted mucus is visible at the stoma opening. He is awake but tiring. What is the most appropriate next step?",
  "choices": ["Attempt orotracheal intubation with video laryngoscopy",
              "Clear the stoma by suction and apply oxygen directly over the stoma",
              "Perform bag-mask ventilation over the mouth and nose with a jaw thrust",
              "Perform an emergency cricothyroidotomy below the hyoid"],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": "After total laryngectomy, the trachea is brought out as an end stoma and has no connection to the mouth or nose. Oxygen or ventilation delivered over the face cannot reach the lungs, and oral intubation is anatomically impossible. The deciding clues are the laryngectomy history and the crusted plug at the stoma. National Tracheostomy Safety Project guidance is to look, listen, and feel at the neck, remove any stoma cover, suction the stoma, and apply oxygen to the stoma. If he needs ventilation, use a pediatric face mask or laryngeal mask applied over the stoma, or pass a small cuffed tube into the stoma. Bedside signs identifying laryngectomy patients help prevent this error.",
  "why_wrong": ["A laryngectomy patient has no larynx and no connection from the mouth to the trachea, so oral intubation cannot work.",
                "Correct.",
                "Face-mask ventilation only inflates the pharynx, which no longer connects to the trachea.",
                "There is no cricothyroid membrane after laryngectomy, and the airway is already open at the stoma."],
  "board_pearl": "A laryngectomy patient breathes only through the neck: oxygenate, suction, and intubate through the stoma, never the mouth.",
  "curveball": "After suction he improves, but he has a tracheoesophageal voice prosthesis, and you notice it is missing from the puncture. Why does this matter?",
  "curveball_answer": "A dislodged prosthesis may have been aspirated into the airway or swallowed, and an open puncture lets saliva and food leak into the trachea. Chest imaging and endoscopy are used to look for an aspirated device, which needs bronchoscopic removal. The puncture should be stented promptly with a catheter or replacement prosthesis so it does not close.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "management"},
