 {"topic": "Deep Neck Abscess Drainage",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-deep-neck-abscess-drainage",
  "stem": "A 24-year-old man has 4 days of worsening right sore throat, a muffled voice, and trismus with a 2.5 cm opening. The right soft palate bulges above the tonsil, and the uvula is deviated to the left. He is handling secretions and has no stridor. You plan needle aspiration in the emergency department after topical and local anesthesia. Which technique is safest?",
  "choices": ["Direct the needle laterally toward the deepest point of fluctuance in the lateral pharyngeal wall",
              "Aspirate low at the inferior pole of the tonsil near the tongue base, where the abscess usually collects",
              "Advance the needle until a loss of resistance is felt, then aspirate as you withdraw",
              "Enter at the superior pole bulge in a sagittal plane, with depth limited by a trimmed cap"],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": "Peritonsillar abscesses usually collect at the superior pole, between the tonsil capsule and the superior constrictor. The internal carotid artery lies posterolateral to the tonsillar fossa, so a needle angled laterally or pushed deep risks vascular injury. The deciding point is safe trajectory: enter at the point of maximal bulge in the soft palate above the tonsil, keep the needle in a sagittal, front-to-back plane, and limit depth by cutting the needle cap so only about a centimeter protrudes. A negative superior aspirate can be followed by a cautious attempt at the middle pole. Intraoral ultrasound can help confirm and localize the collection when the exam is uncertain. Aspirates are sent for culture when patients are high risk or fail treatment.",
  "why_wrong": ["Angling laterally aims the needle toward the internal carotid artery.",
                "Peritonsillar abscesses usually collect at the superior pole, and blind deep aspiration low in the fossa is less likely to find pus.",
                "Unguarded deep insertion has no depth limit and puts the great vessels at risk.",
                "Correct."],
  "board_pearl": "Peritonsillar aspiration: superior pole, sagittal plane, guarded depth; never aim lateral.",
  "curveball": "Aspiration at the superior and middle poles is dry, but he remains febrile with trismus. What should you do next?",
  "curveball_answer": "A dry tap does not exclude an abscess, and the collection may be deeper or the process may still be cellulitis. Contrast CT or intraoral ultrasound can show whether a drainable collection exists, including spread to the parapharyngeal space. Continue IV antibiotics and drain any collection that is found.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "surgery"},
 # 15
 {"topic": "Esophageal Perforation / Cervical Mediastinitis",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-esophageal-perforation-cervical-mediastinitis",
  "stem": "A 70-year-old man with large anterior cervical osteophytes undergoes rigid esophagoscopy for a suspected cervical esophageal lesion. The surgeon meets firm resistance while passing the scope behind the cricoid, and the procedure is stopped. Four hours later the patient has neck pain, odynophagia, and crepitus above the clavicles. A student asks where an instrumental perforation is most likely in this setting. Where is it?",
  "choices": ["Posterior hypopharyngeal wall just above the cricopharyngeus",
              "The anterior esophageal wall where the left mainstem bronchus crosses",
              "The left posterolateral wall of the distal esophagus above the diaphragm",
              "The mid esophagus at the indentation made by the aortic arch"],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": "Most instrumental perforations of the upper aerodigestive tract occur at the posterior hypopharyngeal wall just above the cricopharyngeus. This area, Killian's triangle, lies between the oblique thyropharyngeus and the horizontal cricopharyngeus and has thin muscular support. The cricopharyngeus is also the narrowest point of the esophagus, and the scope tip is pressed against the posterior wall here. The deciding clues are anterior cervical osteophytes, which push the posterior wall forward against the scope, and resistance at the cricoid level. Cervical crepitus and neck pain fit a cervical perforation. The left posterolateral distal esophagus is the typical site of spontaneous Boerhaave rupture after vomiting, not instrumental injury.",
  "why_wrong": ["Correct.",
                "The bronchial crossing is a site of narrowing, but it is not the most common site of instrumental perforation.",
                "The distal left posterolateral wall is the classic site of Boerhaave rupture after forceful vomiting.",
                "The aortic arch indentation is a narrowing that can hold foreign bodies but is not the most common instrumental site."],
  "board_pearl": "Instrumental perforation most often happens at Killian's triangle on the posterior hypopharyngeal wall, especially with cervical osteophytes.",
  "curveball": "A water-soluble contrast study and CT confirm a small contained leak with no abscess, and he is stable. What is reasonable initial management?",
  "curveball_answer": "A small, contained cervical perforation in a stable patient can often be managed nonoperatively. This includes nothing by mouth, broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, nutrition by a route that bypasses the leak, and close monitoring for fever, sepsis, or a developing collection. Worsening signs call for drainage of the neck.",
  "learning_stage": "foundation",
  "focus": "anatomy"},
 # 16
 {"topic": "ENT Perioperative Anesthesia / Difficult Airway Planning",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-ent-perioperative-anesthesia-difficult-airway-planning",
  "stem": "A healthy 23-year-old rugby player is extubated after septoplasty and develops laryngospasm, making vigorous inspiratory efforts against a closed glottis for about a minute before it is broken with positive pressure and a small dose of propofol. Twenty minutes later in recovery he coughs pink frothy sputum and desaturates to 88% on a face mask. He has bilateral crackles, chest radiograph shows bilateral alveolar opacities, and bedside echocardiography shows normal heart function. He received no blood products. What is the most likely mechanism of his lung injury?",
  "choices": ["Aspiration of gastric contents during emergence",
              "Very negative intrathoracic pressure from forced inspiration",
              "Cardiogenic pulmonary edema from an undiagnosed cardiomyopathy",
              "An anaphylactic reaction to latex or a perioperative antibiotic"],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": "This is negative-pressure pulmonary edema, also called post-obstructive pulmonary edema. Forceful inspiration against a closed glottis creates very negative intrathoracic pressure, which increases venous return and pulmonary capillary pressure and pulls fluid into the alveoli. The deciding clues are the witnessed laryngospasm, onset within minutes, pink frothy sputum, and normal cardiac function. Young, muscular patients who can generate large inspiratory forces are at higher risk. Treatment is supportive, with oxygen and positive airway pressure such as CPAP or, if needed, reintubation with PEEP. It usually resolves within a day or two.",
  "why_wrong": ["Aspiration usually follows vomiting or regurgitation and causes dependent infiltrates, which are not described here.",
                "Correct.",
                "Bedside echocardiography shows normal heart function, which argues against a cardiogenic cause.",
                "He has no urticaria, hypotension, or bronchospasm, and anaphylaxis would not explain the timing with laryngospasm."],
  "board_pearl": "Pink frothy sputum minutes after laryngospasm in a young muscular patient is negative-pressure pulmonary edema; treat with oxygen and positive pressure.",
  "curveball": "The recovery nurse asks whether to give a large dose of IV furosemide. How do you respond?",
  "curveball_answer": "The mainstay of treatment is supportive care with oxygen and positive pressure, and most patients improve quickly. Diuretics are sometimes used, but their benefit is uncertain and they can cause hypovolemia in a young patient who is not fluid overloaded. Focus on oxygenation, monitor closely, and reintubate if he cannot maintain adequate saturation.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "complication"},
