 {"topic": "Hemostasis / Coagulopathy / Antithrombotic Management in ENT",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-hemostasis-coagulopathy-antithrombotic-management-in-ent",
  "stem": "A 76-year-old man taking dabigatran for atrial fibrillation has brisk posterior epistaxis that continues despite a posterior balloon pack. His blood pressure is 84/50 after crystalloid, and he is being transfused. His last dose was 4 hours ago, and his creatinine clearance is 35 mL/min. Along with resuscitation and airway protection, which reversal agent is most appropriate?",
  "choices": ["Andexanet alfa",
              "Idarucizumab",
              "Vitamin K with fresh frozen plasma",
              "Protamine sulfate"],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": "Idarucizumab is a humanized antibody fragment that binds dabigatran with high affinity and rapidly reverses its anticoagulant effect. It is indicated for life-threatening bleeding or urgent surgery in patients taking dabigatran. The deciding clues are the specific drug, a recent dose, impaired kidney function that prolongs dabigatran clearance, and hemodynamic instability. Andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors such as apixaban and rivaroxaban, not direct thrombin inhibitors. Vitamin K and plasma reverse warfarin, and protamine reverses heparin. If idarucizumab is unavailable, dabigatran can be removed by hemodialysis, and prothrombin complex concentrate may be considered.",
  "why_wrong": ["Andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors and has no effect on dabigatran, a direct thrombin inhibitor.",
                "Correct.",
                "Vitamin K and plasma reverse vitamin K antagonists such as warfarin and do not reliably reverse dabigatran.",
                "Protamine reverses unfractionated heparin and, partially, low-molecular-weight heparin, not dabigatran."],
  "board_pearl": "Dabigatran is reversed with idarucizumab; apixaban and rivaroxaban are reversed with andexanet alfa or prothrombin complex concentrate.",
  "curveball": "After reversal and embolization the bleeding stops. When should anticoagulation be restarted?",
  "curveball_answer": "The timing balances rebleeding risk against his stroke risk from atrial fibrillation and should be decided with cardiology. Once hemostasis is secure and the cause is treated, anticoagulation is usually resumed within days rather than stopped permanently. Kidney function should guide the choice and dose of agent.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "pharmacology"},
 # 18
 {"topic": "ENT Fluids / Electrolytes / Nutrition",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-ent-fluids-electrolytes-nutrition",
  "stem": "A 45-year-old man is on postoperative day 1 after endoscopic endonasal resection of a pituitary macroadenoma. Over the past 4 hours his urine output has been 300 to 400 mL per hour, and he is very thirsty. Serum sodium has risen from 141 to 149 mmol/L, urine specific gravity is 1.002, and urine osmolality is 150 mOsm/kg. He received no mannitol and his glucose is normal. What is the most appropriate management?",
  "choices": ["Restrict fluids to 1 L per day to reduce urine output",
              "Give 3% hypertonic saline to correct the volume loss",
              "Replace all urine output with normal saline milliliter for milliliter",
              "Give desmopressin and monitor sodium closely"],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": "High-volume dilute urine with a rising serum sodium after pituitary surgery is central diabetes insipidus, caused by injury to the posterior pituitary or stalk and loss of antidiuretic hormone. The deciding clues are polyuria, very low urine specific gravity and osmolality, rising sodium, and the absence of mannitol or glucose diuresis. Desmopressin replaces the missing hormone, and an alert patient with intact thirst should be allowed to drink. Sodium must be checked frequently, because postoperative DI can follow a triphasic course. An initial DI phase may be followed about a week later by inappropriate antidiuretic hormone release with hyponatremia, and then sometimes by permanent DI. For this reason, desmopressin is often given as needed rather than on a fixed schedule early on.",
  "why_wrong": ["Fluid restriction in DI worsens dehydration and hypernatremia.",
                "Hypertonic saline would raise sodium further and is used for symptomatic hyponatremia, not DI.",
                "Matching urine output with isotonic saline adds a sodium load and does not stop the free water loss.",
                "Correct."],
  "board_pearl": "Polyuria with dilute urine and rising sodium after pituitary surgery is DI; treat with desmopressin and watch for the later SIADH phase.",
  "curveball": "On postoperative day 7, after discharge, he presents with headache and nausea and a sodium of 126 mmol/L. What is going on?",
  "curveball_answer": "This fits the second phase of the triphasic response, in which damaged posterior pituitary neurons release stored antidiuretic hormone, causing SIADH-like hyponatremia. Desmopressin should be held, fluids restricted, and sodium corrected carefully to avoid overly rapid correction. Patients should be warned about this delayed phase at discharge.",
  "learning_stage": "mastery",
  "focus": "management"},
