 {"topic": "Antimicrobial Stewardship in Otolaryngology",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-antimicrobial-stewardship-in-otolaryngology",
  "stem": "A 3-year-old boy had tympanostomy tubes placed 4 months ago for recurrent acute otitis media. He now has 3 days of purulent drainage from the left tube. He is afebrile, playful, and eating normally, and there is no redness or swelling of the auricle or postauricular skin. The tube is in place and patent. What is the most appropriate treatment?",
  "choices": ["Topical quinolone ear drops alone",
              "Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate for 10 days",
              "Oral amoxicillin combined with topical antibiotic drops",
              "Removal of the tube in the operating room"],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": "The AAO-HNS tympanostomy tube guideline recommends topical antibiotic drops alone, without oral antibiotics, for uncomplicated acute tube otorrhea. Drops deliver far higher drug concentrations to the middle ear through the open tube, work at least as well as oral antibiotics, and cause fewer systemic side effects. The deciding clues are an otherwise well, afebrile child without spread of infection beyond the ear. Nonototoxic quinolone drops, with or without a steroid, are used rather than aminoglycoside drops. Cleaning drainage from the canal helps the drops reach the tube. Oral antibiotics are reserved for children who are systemically ill, have cellulitis spreading beyond the ear, or fail topical therapy.",
  "why_wrong": ["Correct.",
                "Oral antibiotics alone are less effective than drops for uncomplicated tube otorrhea and add systemic side effects.",
                "Adding oral antibiotics to drops offers no proven benefit for uncomplicated tube otorrhea.",
                "A patent tube with uncomplicated otorrhea does not need removal; it is treated with drops."],
  "board_pearl": "Uncomplicated tympanostomy tube otorrhea is treated with topical quinolone drops alone, not oral antibiotics.",
  "curveball": "The drainage persists despite 10 days of drops used correctly. What would you do next?",
  "curveball_answer": "Clean the ear under the microscope, check that the drops can reach the tube, and send a culture of the drainage to look for resistant organisms such as MRSA or fungi. Treatment is then tailored to the culture result. Persistent drainage with granulation tissue may eventually require tube removal or replacement.",
  "learning_stage": "foundation",
  "focus": "pharmacology"},
 # 20
 {"topic": "Antimicrobial Stewardship in Otolaryngology",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-antimicrobial-stewardship-in-otolaryngology",
  "stem": "A 42-year-old man has 12 days of purulent nasal discharge, nasal obstruction, and maxillary facial pressure that have not improved since a cold. He is otherwise healthy, has no drug allergies, and has not taken antibiotics in the past 3 months. After discussing watchful waiting, you agree to treat him with an antibiotic. He asks for azithromycin because it worked for him years ago. Which regimen is most appropriate?",
  "choices": ["Azithromycin for 5 days, given its convenient dosing",
              "Amoxicillin with or without clavulanate",
              "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for 10 days",
              "Levofloxacin for 10 days"],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": "He meets the criteria for acute bacterial rhinosinusitis because symptoms have lasted 10 days or more without improvement. The AAO-HNS adult sinusitis guideline recommends amoxicillin with or without clavulanate as first-line therapy for 5 to 10 days in most adults when antibiotics are chosen. The deciding point is his request: macrolides and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole are not recommended for empiric treatment because of high rates of resistance in Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae. Respiratory fluoroquinolones are reserved for patients with penicillin allergy or treatment failure, because of their adverse effects and their importance for other infections. Explaining the reason for the choice helps patients accept narrower therapy. If he worsens or fails to improve by 7 days, reassess the diagnosis and consider a change.",
  "why_wrong": ["Macrolides are not recommended for acute bacterial sinusitis because of high pneumococcal resistance.",
                "Correct.",
                "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is not recommended because of high resistance in the common pathogens.",
                "Fluoroquinolones are reserved for penicillin allergy or failure and carry serious adverse effect warnings."],
  "board_pearl": "First-line antibiotic for adult acute bacterial sinusitis is amoxicillin with or without clavulanate; avoid macrolides and TMP-SMX.",
  "curveball": "He returns on day 7 of amoxicillin with worse facial pain and new swelling of the upper eyelid. What is the next step?",
  "curveball_answer": "Periorbital swelling suggests a complication such as preseptal or orbital cellulitis and needs urgent evaluation, not just a change of oral antibiotic. Examine vision, eye movements, and pupils, and obtain contrast CT of the sinuses and orbits. Signs of orbital involvement call for admission, IV antibiotics, and ophthalmology input.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "pharmacology"},
