 {"topic": "Cranial Nerve Examination / Skull Base Localization",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-cranial-nerve-examination-skull-base-localization",
  "stem": "A 41-year-old woman has had left-sided neck pain and headache for 5 days, which began after a minor rear-end car crash. Today she noticed slurred speech. On examination, her tongue deviates to the left when protruded, with mild left tongue weakness. She has a left ptosis and a smaller left pupil, but sweating on her face is symmetric. Facial strength, hearing, and palatal elevation are normal. What is the most appropriate next step?",
  "choices": ["Contrast CT of the neck to look for a parapharyngeal abscess",
              "Electromyography of the tongue to confirm a hypoglossal nerve lesion",
              "Outpatient MRI of the brain in 2 to 3 weeks if symptoms persist",
              "CT angiography or MR angiography of the head and neck"],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": "A partial Horner syndrome with ipsilateral neck pain and headache after neck trauma is internal carotid artery dissection until proven otherwise. The expanding wall hematoma can compress the nearby hypoglossal nerve below the skull base, causing tongue deviation toward the affected side. The deciding clue is preserved facial sweating, because the sympathetic fibers that drive facial sweating travel with the external carotid artery and are spared in internal carotid dissection. Urgent vascular imaging with CT angiography or MR angiography confirms the diagnosis. Dissection carries a risk of stroke, so antithrombotic treatment is started with neurology. Delayed imaging misses the window to prevent an embolic stroke.",
  "why_wrong": ["She has no fever, trismus, or features of infection, and a contrast CT without angiography could miss a dissection.",
                "Electromyography would not change urgent management and does not show the vascular cause.",
                "Delaying imaging for weeks risks a stroke from an undiagnosed carotid dissection.",
                "Correct."],
  "board_pearl": "Painful partial Horner syndrome with ipsilateral hypoglossal palsy after neck trauma means internal carotid dissection: get vascular imaging now.",
  "curveball": "Imaging is normal, and she is later found to have a mass that also causes left vocal fold paralysis and left shoulder weakness. Where is the lesion?",
  "curveball_answer": "Involvement of the vagus, spinal accessory, and hypoglossal nerves with Horner syndrome localizes to the retrostyloid parapharyngeal space just below the skull base, sometimes called Villaret syndrome. Contrast MRI of the skull base and neck is the best study. Paragangliomas, schwannomas, and metastatic nodes are common causes here.",
  "learning_stage": "mastery",
  "focus": "diagnosis"},
 # 22
 {"topic": "Cranial Nerve Examination / Skull Base Localization",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-cranial-nerve-examination-skull-base-localization",
  "stem": "A 68-year-old man with hypertension and diabetes is referred by the emergency department as a possible Bell's palsy. Three hours ago he suddenly developed drooping of the right side of his mouth. He can wrinkle his forehead symmetrically and close both eyes tightly, but the right lower face is weak. His right hand also feels clumsy, and his speech is slightly slurred. What is the most appropriate next step?",
  "choices": ["Start oral prednisolone and valacyclovir and arrange follow-up in 1 week",
              "Arrange electroneuronography at 72 hours to estimate prognosis",
              "Activate the stroke pathway for emergency brain imaging",
              "Order an outpatient MRI of the internal auditory canals with gadolinium"],
  "answer": 2,
  "explanation": "Lower facial weakness that spares the forehead indicates an upper motor neuron lesion. The part of the facial nucleus that supplies the forehead receives input from both cerebral hemispheres, so a unilateral cortical or subcortical lesion leaves forehead movement intact. The deciding clues are forehead sparing, sudden onset, vascular risk factors, hand clumsiness, and dysarthria, which together suggest acute stroke. He is within the treatment window, so the stroke pathway with emergency brain imaging is needed now. A peripheral facial palsy such as Bell's palsy weakens the entire half of the face, including the forehead. Treating a stroke as Bell's palsy loses time for reperfusion treatment.",
  "why_wrong": ["Steroids and antivirals are for Bell's palsy, which involves the forehead, and starting them would delay stroke care.",
                "Electroneuronography assesses peripheral nerve degeneration and is irrelevant for a central lesion.",
                "Correct.",
                "Elective imaging of the internal auditory canal looks for peripheral causes and would miss the time-critical stroke."],
  "board_pearl": "Forehead-sparing facial weakness is central; a peripheral facial palsy weakens the whole hemiface.",
  "curveball": "Another patient has complete right facial weakness including the forehead, plus right-sided hearing loss and vertigo. Where would you localize the lesion?",
  "curveball_answer": "Peripheral facial weakness with hearing loss and vertigo points to the internal auditory canal or cerebellopontine angle, or to the temporal bone, such as in herpes zoster oticus, a temporal bone fracture, or a tumor. Look for vesicles, perform audiometry, and obtain MRI with gadolinium. These findings do not fit Bell's palsy, which should not cause hearing loss.",
  "learning_stage": "foundation",
  "focus": "diagnosis"},
 # 23
 {"topic": "ENT Imaging Fundamentals",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-ent-imaging-fundamentals",
  "stem": "A 58-year-old man with HPV-associated oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma and a single 3 cm level II node completed definitive chemoradiation 4 weeks ago. His primary tumor site looks healed, but a 1.5 cm node remains on examination. The medical oncologist wants a PET-CT now to decide whether he needs a neck dissection. What is the most appropriate imaging strategy?",
  "choices": ["Obtain PET-CT now, at 4 weeks, and perform neck dissection if the node is avid",
              "Perform a planned neck dissection now because a palpable node remains",
              "PET-CT at about 12 weeks; neck dissection only if response is incomplete or equivocal",
              "Obtain contrast MRI of the neck now and repeat it every 4 weeks until the node resolves"],
  "answer": 2,
  "explanation": "Radiation-induced inflammation causes FDG uptake that can be mistaken for residual tumor if PET-CT is done too early. Response assessment PET-CT is therefore obtained at about 12 weeks after the end of chemoradiation. The PET-NECK randomized trial showed that PET-CT surveillance at 12 weeks, with neck dissection only for incomplete or equivocal response, gave survival similar to planned neck dissection while avoiding many operations. The deciding clue is the timing of 4 weeks, when a residual node is common and often still regressing. HPV-associated nodal disease in particular can take months to resolve. A negative 12-week PET-CT has a high negative predictive value, so observation is reasonable.",
  "why_wrong": ["PET-CT at 4 weeks has many false-positive results from post-radiation inflammation.",
                "Planned neck dissection for every residual node leads to many unnecessary operations, as PET-NECK showed.",
                "Correct.",
                "Frequent MRI at short intervals is not a validated response strategy and cannot reliably separate scar from tumor this early."],
  "board_pearl": "Post-chemoradiation PET-CT is timed at about 12 weeks; earlier scans give false positives from inflammation.",
  "curveball": "His 12-week PET-CT shows a 1.2 cm node with low-level FDG uptake similar to background. What next?",
  "curveball_answer": "An equivocal result needs a tailored plan, often discussed at tumor board. Options include repeat imaging at a short interval, ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration, or neck dissection, depending on node size, uptake, and patient preference. A node with clearly increasing uptake or growth should be dissected.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "imaging"},
 # 24
 {"topic": "ENT Imaging Fundamentals",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-ent-imaging-fundamentals",
  "stem": "A 62-year-old man has biopsy-proven nasopharyngeal carcinoma with a mass in the left fossa of Rosenmüller. CT shows no definite cortical erosion of the clivus or skull base. The tumor board needs to know whether there is early invasion of the clival bone marrow, because it would change the T category. Which imaging finding is most sensitive for early marrow invasion?",
  "choices": ["Loss of bright fatty marrow signal on noncontrast T1-weighted MRI",
              "High signal in the clivus on T2-weighted MRI",
              "Restricted diffusion within the clival marrow on diffusion-weighted MRI",
              "Blooming artifact in the clivus on gradient-echo MRI"],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": "In adults, the marrow of the clivus is largely fatty and appears bright on noncontrast T1-weighted MRI. Tumor that infiltrates the marrow replaces the fat and appears as intermediate or low T1 signal, often before CT shows cortical destruction. The deciding point is that CT shows cortical bone well but is insensitive to early marrow invasion, which MRI shows best. T2 signal is less reliable because fat also appears bright on fast spin-echo T2 images, which can mask tumor. Contrast-enhanced fat-suppressed T1 images help, but they are interpreted against the noncontrast T1 baseline. Invasion of the skull base bone upstages nasopharyngeal carcinoma to T3 in AJCC 8th edition.",
  "why_wrong": ["Correct.",
                "Fat is also bright on fast spin-echo T2 images, so T2 signal is less reliable for separating marrow fat from tumor.",
                "Diffusion imaging of the skull base is limited by susceptibility artifact and is not the most sensitive test for marrow invasion.",
                "Gradient-echo blooming indicates blood products or calcification, not marrow invasion by tumor."],
  "board_pearl": "Noncontrast T1 MRI is the key sequence for skull base marrow invasion: replacement of bright marrow fat by darker tumor.",
  "curveball": "In a 6-year-old child, the clivus appears dark on T1-weighted MRI. Does this mean tumor invasion?",
  "curveball_answer": "Not necessarily. Children have red, hematopoietic marrow in the clivus, which appears intermediate to low on T1, and conversion to fatty marrow happens with age. Comparison with other marrow, contrast enhancement patterns, and clinical context are needed before calling it abnormal.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "imaging"},
 # 25
 {"topic": "Wound Healing / Scar Biology in Head & Neck Surgery",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-wound-healing-scar-biology-in-head-neck-surgery",
  "stem": "A 45-year-old man is 3 weeks out from a neck dissection with a well-healed incision. He is a construction worker and asks whether the scar is now as strong as the skin was before surgery, and whether it will continue to strengthen. A medical student on the team asks what happens to the collagen in the scar over the coming year. Which statement best describes the remodeling phase?",
  "choices": ["Type I collagen is gradually replaced by type III collagen, and the scar reaches full original strength by about 6 weeks",
              "Type III collagen is gradually replaced by type I collagen with increasing cross-linking, and strength reaches a maximum of about 80% of normal skin",
              "Collagen production stops after the proliferative phase, so tensile strength reaches its final level at about 3 weeks",
              "Myofibroblasts replace collagen during remodeling, so the scar becomes stronger than the surrounding skin"],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": "Wound healing proceeds through hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, and remodeling. In the proliferative phase, fibroblasts lay down mostly type III collagen. During remodeling, which lasts months to a year or more, type III collagen is replaced by type I collagen, fibers are reorganized, and cross-links form through lysyl oxidase. Tensile strength rises steadily but only reaches about 80% of unwounded skin, and at 3 weeks it is still well below that. The deciding point for this patient is that the scar keeps strengthening for months but never fully matches normal skin. Scar redness and firmness also fade during remodeling, which is why scar revision is usually deferred.",
  "why_wrong": ["The collagen shift goes from type III to type I, and the scar never reaches full original strength.",
                "Correct.",
                "Collagen turnover and cross-linking continue for many months, so strength keeps increasing after 3 weeks.",
                "Myofibroblasts drive wound contraction rather than replacing collagen, and a scar never exceeds normal skin strength."],
  "board_pearl": "Remodeling replaces type III with type I collagen; scars plateau at about 80% of normal skin strength.",
  "curveball": "The same patient's laboratory testing shows severe copper deficiency after years of zinc supplement overuse. Why might this matter for wound strength?",
  "curveball_answer": "Lysyl oxidase, the enzyme that cross-links collagen and elastin, depends on copper. Copper deficiency impairs cross-linking and can weaken the healing wound. Excess zinc intake can cause copper deficiency by blocking copper absorption in the gut.",
  "learning_stage": "foundation",
  "focus": "mechanism"},
