 {"topic": "Wound Healing / Scar Biology in Head & Neck Surgery",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-wound-healing-scar-biology-in-head-neck-surgery",
  "stem": "A 30-year-old woman has a benign lesion excised from her cheek with a layered closure. She asks how to care for the incision at home. Her mother told her to leave it open to the air so a scab forms, and a friend suggested cleaning it daily with hydrogen peroxide and using a triple antibiotic ointment. She has no history of skin allergies. Which advice best supports healing?",
  "choices": ["Leave the incision uncovered and dry so a protective scab forms",
              "Clean the incision with hydrogen peroxide twice daily to prevent infection",
              "Keep the incision moist with a thin layer of plain petrolatum after gentle cleansing",
              "Apply a neomycin-containing antibiotic ointment several times a day"],
  "answer": 2,
  "explanation": "A moist wound environment allows keratinocytes to migrate across the surface quickly, while a dry scab acts as a barrier that slows re-epithelialization. Keeping a clean surgical incision moist with plain petrolatum and gentle cleansing gives faster healing and better cosmetic results. The deciding point is that the incision is a clean, closed wound with low infection risk. Randomized data showed that white petrolatum had infection rates similar to bacitracin ointment for clean dermatologic wounds, without the allergy risk. Hydrogen peroxide is toxic to fibroblasts and keratinocytes and can delay healing. Neomycin is a common cause of allergic contact dermatitis.",
  "why_wrong": ["A dry scab slows the migration of epithelial cells and leads to slower healing and a less favorable scar.",
                "Hydrogen peroxide damages healing cells and should not be used routinely on a closed incision.",
                "Correct.",
                "Neomycin frequently causes allergic contact dermatitis, and routine topical antibiotics add little for clean wounds."],
  "board_pearl": "Clean incisions heal best moist under plain petrolatum; avoid peroxide and routine neomycin.",
  "curveball": "At 2 weeks the scar is pink and slightly raised. She asks whether anything can reduce the chance of a thick scar. What do you suggest?",
  "curveball_answer": "Silicone gel or silicone sheeting, applied daily for several months, is a first-line measure for preventing and treating hypertrophic scars. Sun protection helps prevent darkening of the scar while it is still maturing. Gentle massage can be added once the wound is fully healed.",
  "learning_stage": "foundation",
  "focus": "management"},
