 {"topic": "Grafts / Implants / Biomaterials in ENT",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-grafts-implants-biomaterials-in-ent",
  "stem": "A 66-year-old woman has a 2 cm defect of the anterior lamella of the left lower eyelid after Mohs excision of a basal cell carcinoma. Local flap options are limited, so a skin graft is planned. The resident proposes a thin split-thickness graft from the thigh because it takes more reliably. The attending prefers a full-thickness graft from the upper eyelid or postauricular skin. Which property is the main reason to prefer a full-thickness graft here?",
  "choices": ["A full-thickness graft survives better than a split-thickness graft on a poorly vascularized bed",
              "A thin split-thickness graft has less secondary contraction and would protect the eyelid position",
              "A full-thickness graft has less primary contraction when harvested, so it fills the defect more exactly",
              "A full-thickness graft carries the whole dermis, so it undergoes much less secondary contraction during healing"],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": "Primary contraction is the immediate recoil of a graft after harvest because of its elastic fibers, and it is greater in full-thickness grafts. Secondary contraction occurs as the graft heals on its bed and is driven by myofibroblasts; it is greatest in thin split-thickness grafts and least in full-thickness grafts. The deciding point is the lower eyelid, where secondary contraction pulls the lid down and causes ectropion. A full-thickness graft also gives better color, texture, and thickness match from upper eyelid or postauricular donor sites. Split-thickness grafts do take more reliably on marginal beds because they need less nutrition, but that advantage matters less on the well-vascularized face. Good graft take still requires a healthy bed and a bolster to prevent shearing and fluid collection.",
  "why_wrong": ["Thinner split-thickness grafts, not full-thickness grafts, survive better on poorly vascularized beds.",
                "Thin split-thickness grafts have the most secondary contraction, which would increase the risk of ectropion.",
                "Full-thickness grafts have more primary contraction, not less, so they must be cut generously.",
                "Correct."],
  "board_pearl": "Full-thickness grafts: more primary, less secondary contraction; thin split-thickness grafts: less primary, more secondary contraction.",
  "curveball": "On day 5 the graft looks dusky but intact under the bolster. How does a skin graft survive in its first days, before it has a blood supply?",
  "curveball_answer": "In the first 24 to 48 hours the graft survives by plasmatic imbibition, absorbing nutrients from the wound bed. Inosculation follows, as graft and bed capillaries connect, and true revascularization with new vessel ingrowth occurs over the following days. Shearing, hematoma, or seroma during this period separates the graft from the bed and causes failure.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "surgery"},
 # 28
 {"topic": "Grafts / Implants / Biomaterials in ENT",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-grafts-implants-biomaterials-in-ent",
  "stem": "A 9-year-old boy with a repaired unilateral cleft lip and palate is undergoing secondary alveolar bone grafting before eruption of his permanent canine. The surgeon harvests cancellous bone from the iliac crest rather than using demineralized bone matrix or a hydroxyapatite substitute. A resident asks what fresh autologous cancellous bone offers that the alternatives cannot. Which property is unique to it?",
  "choices": ["Osteoconduction, providing a scaffold for new bone to grow along",
              "Osteoinduction, through bone morphogenetic proteins that recruit stem cells",
              "Osteogenesis, from living osteoprogenitor cells that form new bone directly",
              "Osseointegration, forming a direct bond with titanium implants"],
  "answer": 2,
  "explanation": "Bone grafts work through three mechanisms. Osteoconduction is a scaffold for new bone, osteoinduction is the recruitment and differentiation of host stem cells by growth factors such as bone morphogenetic proteins, and osteogenesis is new bone formed directly by living cells carried in the graft. Fresh autologous cancellous bone provides all three, but osteogenesis is unique to it, because it carries viable osteoprogenitor cells. Demineralized bone matrix is osteoinductive and osteoconductive, and hydroxyapatite is only osteoconductive. The deciding point is the need for a graft that will become living bone through which the canine can erupt. That is why iliac crest cancellous bone remains the standard for alveolar cleft grafting.",
  "why_wrong": ["Osteoconduction is shared by allografts and synthetic scaffolds such as hydroxyapatite, so it is not unique to autograft.",
                "Osteoinduction is also provided by demineralized bone matrix and recombinant bone morphogenetic protein.",
                "Correct.",
                "Osseointegration describes the bond between bone and a titanium implant, not a graft property."],
  "board_pearl": "Only fresh autograft is osteogenic; allograft matrix is osteoinductive and conductive, and hydroxyapatite is only osteoconductive.",
  "curveball": "Why is alveolar cleft grafting usually timed before the permanent canine erupts?",
  "curveball_answer": "Grafting before canine eruption, often in mixed dentition around ages 8 to 11, gives the canine living bone to erupt through and stabilizes the maxillary arch. It also supports the alar base and closes the oronasal fistula. Waiting until after eruption risks poor periodontal support for the teeth next to the cleft.",
  "learning_stage": "foundation",
  "focus": "mechanism"},
 # 29
 {"topic": "Systemic / Granulomatous Disease Manifestations in ENT",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-systemic-granulomatous-disease-manifestations-in-ent",
  "stem": "A 36-year-old man has 1 year of nasal crusting, foul odor, and progressive nasal obstruction. Examination shows a large septal perforation, loss of much of the inferior turbinates, and a perforation of the hard palate. Urinalysis, creatinine, and chest CT are normal. ANCA testing is positive in a perinuclear pattern, with antibodies to human neutrophil elastase. Biopsies show mucosal ulceration and nonspecific inflammation with necrosis, without granulomas or vasculitis. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  "choices": ["Cocaine-induced midline destructive lesion",
              "Granulomatosis with polyangiitis limited to the head and neck",
              "Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type",
              "Sinonasal sarcoidosis"],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": "Cocaine can cause extensive midline destruction of the septum, turbinates, and palate that closely mimics granulomatosis with polyangiitis. The deciding clues are a hard palate perforation, which is uncommon in GPA, antibodies to human neutrophil elastase, which are characteristic of cocaine-related disease, and biopsies without granulomas or vasculitis. ANCA can be positive in cocaine users, particularly with levamisole-adulterated cocaine, so a positive ANCA alone does not establish GPA. A careful, nonjudgmental substance use history and urine toxicology help confirm the diagnosis. Immunosuppression does not help, and abstinence is the main treatment. Reconstruction should wait until abstinence is sustained and the disease is quiescent.",
  "why_wrong": ["Correct.",
                "GPA usually shows PR3-ANCA and biopsy features of vasculitis or granulomas and rarely perforates the hard palate.",
                "NK/T-cell lymphoma is EBV-associated with atypical lymphoid cells on biopsy, which were not found.",
                "Sarcoidosis shows noncaseating granulomas on biopsy and does not usually cause palatal perforation."],
  "board_pearl": "Midline destruction with palatal perforation, anti-elastase ANCA, and no vasculitis on biopsy points to cocaine, not GPA.",
  "curveball": "He continues using cocaine and asks for a palatal obturator or surgical repair now. What do you advise?",
  "curveball_answer": "A palatal obturator can improve speech and eating while disease is active and can be adjusted as the defect changes. Surgical repair should wait until he has been abstinent for a sustained period, because ongoing use leads to graft and flap failure and further destruction. Referral for addiction treatment is part of care.",
  "learning_stage": "mastery",
  "focus": "diagnosis"},
 # 30
 {"topic": "Systemic / Granulomatous Disease Manifestations in ENT",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-systemic-granulomatous-disease-manifestations-in-ent",
  "stem": "A 48-year-old man originally from Southeast Asia has 2 months of progressive nasal obstruction, foul crusting, night sweats, and weight loss. Endoscopy shows necrotic ulceration of the septum extending to the hard palate. ANCA testing is negative. A superficial biopsy of the ulcer shows only necrosis and acute inflammation, and the referring team plans to start high-dose prednisone for presumed vasculitis. What is the most appropriate next step?",
  "choices": ["Start high-dose prednisone and repeat the biopsy if he does not respond",
              "Check serum angiotensin-converting enzyme and calcium levels",
              "Culture the tissue for Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis",
              "Deep repeat biopsy with CD56 staining and EBV in situ hybridization"],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": "Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type, causes destructive midline lesions with extensive necrosis, so superficial biopsies are often nondiagnostic. The deciding clues are rapid midline destruction, constitutional symptoms, negative ANCA, and a necrosis-only biopsy. Deep biopsies from viable tissue at the edge of the lesion, sent fresh for flow cytometry and stained for CD56, cytotoxic markers, and EBV-encoded RNA, establish the diagnosis. The tumor is strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus, which is found in nearly all cases. Starting steroids before diagnosis can partly treat lymphoma, distort pathology, and delay care. Plasma EBV DNA can be used as a tumor marker once the diagnosis is made.",
  "why_wrong": ["Empiric steroids can obscure lymphoma on later biopsy and delay definitive treatment.",
                "ACE and calcium are nonspecific and do not address the leading concern of lymphoma.",
                "Rhinoscleroma is a chronic granulomatous infection that does not fit this rapid necrotic course with B symptoms.",
                "Correct."],
  "board_pearl": "Necrotic midline destruction with a nondiagnostic biopsy needs deep repeat biopsy with EBV in situ hybridization before any steroids.",
  "curveball": "The biopsy confirms NK/T-cell lymphoma. What is the general approach to treatment?",
  "curveball_answer": "Localized disease is usually treated with radiation combined with asparaginase-based chemotherapy, coordinated by hematology and radiation oncology. Anthracycline-based regimens such as CHOP perform poorly because the tumor expresses drug-resistance proteins. Staging includes PET-CT, bone marrow evaluation, and plasma EBV DNA.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "workup"},
