 {"topic": "Laser / Energy Safety in Otolaryngology",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-laser-energy-safety-in-otolaryngology",
  "stem": "A laryngologist regularly performs CO2 laser and microdebrider excision of recurrent respiratory papillomatosis. A new scrub technician asks whether the laser plume is harmful and whether a standard surgical mask is enough protection. The surgeon mentions that there have been case reports of laryngeal papillomas in surgeons who treat these patients. Which measure best reduces the staff's exposure to infectious material in the plume?",
  "choices": ["A standard surgical mask, since it filters the particles in laser plume",
              "A smoke evacuator held close to the treatment site, combined with fit-tested N95 respirators",
              "Laser-specific eye protection, since the main risk of plume is to the eyes",
              "Lowering the laser power, since low-power plumes do not contain viral DNA"],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": "Laser and electrosurgical plume contains fine particles, chemical byproducts, and, when treating HPV-related lesions, viral DNA. Case reports have described laryngeal papillomatosis in surgeons who treat HPV-related lesions, so plume is treated as potentially infectious. The deciding point is that standard surgical masks do not effectively filter the small particles in plume. The most effective control is a smoke evacuator with its intake close to the treatment site, removing plume at the source. Fit-tested N95 respirators add personal protection for the team. Some experts also suggest HPV vaccination for staff who frequently treat HPV-related disease.",
  "why_wrong": ["Standard surgical masks do not reliably filter the fine particles in laser plume.",
                "Correct.",
                "Eye protection prevents beam injury, but it does not protect against inhaled plume.",
                "Plume at any power can carry particulate and viral material, so lowering power is not adequate protection."],
  "board_pearl": "HPV DNA can be present in laser plume: use local smoke evacuation at the source and N95 respirators.",
  "curveball": "During the same case, the anesthesiologist asks why the patient's eyes are covered with wet gauze. What risk is being managed?",
  "curveball_answer": "A stray or reflected CO2 laser beam can burn the cornea, and wet gauze or eye pads absorb that energy because the CO2 wavelength is absorbed by water. Staff wear clear glass or polycarbonate lenses, which block the CO2 wavelength. Other lasers, such as Nd:YAG, penetrate to the retina and need wavelength-specific filtered goggles.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "complication"},
 # 32
 {"topic": "Laser / Energy Safety in Otolaryngology",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-laser-energy-safety-in-otolaryngology",
  "stem": "A 60-year-old man is undergoing excision of a skin cancer of the left temple and preauricular area under general anesthesia with a cuffed endotracheal tube and 30% oxygen. The skin and scalp were prepped with a chlorhexidine-alcohol solution, and draping began immediately afterward. The solution pooled in his sideburn hair and under the drape. When the surgeon uses monopolar cautery to make the incision, a flash fire ignites under the drapes. Which measure would most directly have prevented this fire?",
  "choices": ["Using bipolar cautery only for the entire operation",
              "Lowering the inspired oxygen through the cuffed tube to 21%",
              "Letting the alcohol prep dry fully, without pooling, before draping",
              "Adding an extra layer of drapes around the operative field to contain sparks"],
  "answer": 2,
  "explanation": "A surgical fire needs three elements: an ignition source, a fuel, and an oxidizer. Alcohol-based skin preps are highly flammable until fully dry, and pooled solution or vapors trapped under drapes or in hair can ignite from an electrosurgical spark. The deciding clues are draping immediately after prep and pooling in hair, with the airway sealed by a cuffed tube so there was no open oxygen source at the field. Allowing the prep to dry for the manufacturer's recommended time, longer in hair, and wicking away pooled solution removes the fuel. Bipolar cautery reduces but does not eliminate spark risk. Extra drapes can trap flammable vapors and make the problem worse.",
  "why_wrong": ["Bipolar cautery lowers but does not eliminate the ignition risk, and it does not remove the flammable fuel.",
                "With a cuffed tube there is little oxygen at the surgical field, so lowering the inspired oxygen would not address the cause.",
                "Correct.",
                "Additional drapes can trap alcohol vapors and increase the fire risk."],
  "board_pearl": "Alcohol-based prep is fuel until it is completely dry: wait the full drying time, longer in hair, and never drape over pooled prep.",
  "curveball": "If the same procedure were done under sedation with oxygen by nasal cannula, what additional fire prevention steps would apply?",
  "curveball_answer": "Open oxygen near the face adds an oxidizer to the field, which greatly increases fire risk. Use the lowest oxygen concentration that keeps saturation acceptable, stop or reduce oxygen for about a minute before using cautery near the face, and arrange drapes so oxygen does not pool. If higher oxygen is needed, securing the airway with a tube or laryngeal mask is safer.",
  "learning_stage": "foundation",
  "focus": "complication"},
 # 33
 {"topic": "Common ENT Consult Triage / Disposition",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-common-ent-consult-triage-disposition",
  "stem": "A 14-year-old boy has had 10 days of nasal congestion and purulent drainage, and over the past 3 days he has developed a soft, tender swelling over his forehead with headache and a low-grade fever. He is alert, with a normal neurologic examination and no visual changes. Contrast CT of the sinuses shows frontal sinus opacification, erosion of the anterior table, and a subperiosteal fluid collection over the forehead. The emergency physician asks whether he can be discharged on oral antibiotics with clinic follow-up. What is the most appropriate disposition?",
  "choices": ["Admit for IV antibiotics, brain MRI, and surgical drainage",
              "Discharge on oral amoxicillin-clavulanate with clinic follow-up in 1 week",
              "Aspirate the forehead collection in the emergency department and discharge on oral antibiotics",
              "Schedule elective endoscopic sinus surgery in 2 weeks after a course of oral antibiotics"],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": "A doughy forehead swelling from frontal sinusitis with anterior table erosion and a subperiosteal abscess is Pott's puffy tumor, a frontal bone osteomyelitis. It is most common in adolescents, whose frontal sinuses and diploic veins are actively developing, and it is frequently associated with intracranial complications such as epidural abscess, subdural empyema, and brain abscess. The deciding point is that a normal neurologic examination does not exclude these complications. Contrast MRI is more sensitive than CT for intracranial spread. Management is admission, IV antibiotics that cover streptococci, staphylococci, and anaerobes, and surgical drainage of the sinus and collection. Neurosurgery joins if there is intracranial involvement.",
  "why_wrong": ["Correct.",
                "Outpatient oral treatment of frontal osteomyelitis risks missed or progressive intracranial infection.",
                "Aspiration alone does not treat the sinus osteomyelitis or exclude intracranial spread.",
                "Delaying surgery for weeks is unsafe in an established osteomyelitis with abscess."],
  "board_pearl": "Forehead swelling with frontal sinusitis in a teenager is Pott's puffy tumor: admit, get MRI for intracranial spread, and drain.",
  "curveball": "MRI shows a small frontal epidural abscess with no mass effect, and he remains neurologically intact. Does he need a craniotomy?",
  "curveball_answer": "Small epidural abscesses without mass effect in a neurologically intact patient are sometimes treated with sinus drainage and prolonged IV antibiotics, with close neurosurgical follow-up and repeat imaging. Larger collections, mass effect, or neurologic decline call for neurosurgical drainage. The decision is made jointly with neurosurgery.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "workup"},
 # 34
 {"topic": "Evidence Interpretation / Outcomes Research",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-evidence-interpretation-outcomes-research",
  "stem": "A 50-year-old man with moderate obstructive sleep apnea asks about a randomized trial that compared a new airway surgery with continued CPAP. In the CPAP arm, 30% of patients stopped using CPAP and underwent the surgery outside the trial during follow-up. A per-protocol analysis showed that surgery improved sleepiness scores more than CPAP, but the intention-to-treat analysis showed no significant difference. Which interpretation is most appropriate?",
  "choices": ["The per-protocol result is more valid because it reflects the treatment patients actually received",
              "The trial is invalid because crossover makes any comparison impossible",
              "The difference between analyses proves that CPAP is harmful to those who stop using it",
              "Intention-to-treat is primary because it preserves randomization"],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": "Intention-to-treat analysis compares patients in the groups to which they were randomized, regardless of what treatment they actually received. It preserves the balance of known and unknown confounders created by randomization and estimates the real-world effect of offering a treatment. Per-protocol analysis includes only patients who followed their assigned treatment, so it compares groups that are no longer randomized. The deciding clue is heavy crossover from CPAP to surgery, since patients who abandon CPAP differ systematically from those who keep using it. That selection makes the per-protocol result vulnerable to bias. Per-protocol results remain useful as supporting analyses, especially in noninferiority trials, but superiority conclusions usually rest on intention to treat.",
  "why_wrong": ["Per-protocol analysis breaks randomization, so it is more prone to selection bias, not more valid.",
                "Crossover weakens a trial and biases the intention-to-treat result toward no difference, but it does not make it uninterpretable.",
                "Neither analysis shows harm from CPAP; the difference reflects how patients were grouped.",
                "Correct."],
  "board_pearl": "Intention-to-treat preserves randomization and is the primary analysis for superiority; per-protocol analyses risk selection bias.",
  "curveball": "Why might intention-to-treat be considered less conservative in a noninferiority trial?",
  "curveball_answer": "Crossover and nonadherence make groups more alike and push the intention-to-treat result toward no difference. In a superiority trial that is conservative, but in a noninferiority trial it can make an inferior treatment look noninferior. For this reason, noninferiority trials usually report both intention-to-treat and per-protocol analyses and look for consistent results.",
  "learning_stage": "mastery",
  "focus": "counseling"},
