 {"topic": "Evidence Interpretation / Outcomes Research",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-evidence-interpretation-outcomes-research",
  "stem": "A 45-year-old woman with sudden sensorineural hearing loss asks about a large randomized trial of a new drug she read about online. The trial found no difference in hearing recovery overall. However, the authors reported 12 subgroup analyses, and in women under 50 the drug showed a benefit with a p value of 0.03; the interaction test was not reported. She fits that subgroup and asks whether the drug will work for her. Which interpretation is most appropriate?",
  "choices": ["The subgroup result proves the drug works in women under 50 because the p value is below 0.05",
              "The subgroup result should be treated as hypothesis-generating, because multiple comparisons make a chance finding likely",
              "The overall negative result is invalid because the drug clearly works in at least one subgroup",
              "The subgroup result is more reliable than the overall result because it is more specific to her"],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": "When many subgroups are tested, some will reach statistical significance by chance alone. With 12 comparisons at a threshold of 0.05, the chance of at least one false-positive finding is substantial. The deciding clues are an overall negative trial, many subgroup analyses, a borderline p value, and no reported test of interaction. A credible subgroup effect is prespecified with a plausible rationale, shown by a significant interaction test, and replicated in other studies. Without these features, the result should be viewed as hypothesis-generating. She should be counseled that the best estimate of the drug's effect for her is the overall trial result.",
  "why_wrong": ["A p value below 0.05 in one of many subgroups can easily arise by chance and does not prove an effect.",
                "Correct.",
                "A chance subgroup finding does not overturn the main result of a well-conducted trial.",
                "Smaller subgroups have less power and more random error, so their results are less reliable, not more."],
  "board_pearl": "Subgroup findings in a negative trial are hypothesis-generating unless they are prespecified, supported by an interaction test, and replicated.",
  "curveball": "If the subgroup analysis had been prespecified with a strong biological rationale and a significant interaction test, how would that change your counseling?",
  "curveball_answer": "Those features make a true subgroup effect more credible, although replication in another trial is still ideal. You could tell her the evidence suggests a possible benefit in her group, while explaining the uncertainty. The final decision would also weigh the drug's side effects, cost, and her preferences.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "counseling"},
 # 36
 {"topic": "Pain Management in the Head & Neck Patient",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-pain-management-in-the-head-neck-patient",
  "stem": "A 58-year-old woman has 3 months of paroxysmal, stabbing pain deep in the left side of her throat that radiates to the left ear. Each attack lasts seconds and is triggered by swallowing, yawning, and talking. Examination, including flexible laryngoscopy and palpation of the tonsillar fossa, is normal. Spraying topical lidocaine on the left tonsil and pharyngeal wall temporarily abolishes the attacks. MRI of the brain and neck shows no mass. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?",
  "choices": ["Carbamazepine, titrated to effect",
              "A scheduled long-acting opioid",
              "Microvascular decompression of the glossopharyngeal nerve as initial therapy",
              "Left tonsillectomy to remove a possible pain trigger"],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": "Brief, electric, stabbing pain in the throat and ear triggered by swallowing, yawning, or talking is classic glossopharyngeal neuralgia. Temporary relief with topical anesthesia of the tonsil and pharynx supports the diagnosis by blocking the trigger zone. The deciding clues are the paroxysmal character, typical triggers, normal examination, and normal MRI, which excludes compressive tumors. First-line treatment, as with trigeminal neuralgia, is carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine, which stabilize neuronal sodium channels. Opioids are generally ineffective for paroxysmal neuropathic pain. Microvascular decompression or other surgery is reserved for patients who fail or cannot tolerate medical therapy.",
  "why_wrong": ["Correct.",
                "Opioids are poorly effective for paroxysmal neuralgic pain and carry dependence and sedation risks.",
                "Surgical decompression is reserved for medically refractory cases, not used first.",
                "Tonsillectomy does not treat neuralgia when the examination and imaging show no structural cause."],
  "board_pearl": "Swallow-triggered stabbing throat and ear pain relieved by topical tonsil anesthesia is glossopharyngeal neuralgia; start carbamazepine.",
  "curveball": "Some patients with glossopharyngeal neuralgia faint during attacks. Why?",
  "curveball_answer": "The glossopharyngeal nerve carries afferent fibers from the carotid sinus, and intense discharge during an attack can trigger a reflex vagal response with bradycardia, asystole, or hypotension. This rare form causes syncope during attacks. It needs cardiac evaluation, and some patients need a pacemaker or surgery to control the reflex.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "pharmacology"},
 # 37
 {"topic": "Pain Management in the Head & Neck Patient",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-pain-management-in-the-head-neck-patient",
  "stem": "A 74-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease (eGFR 32) takes pregabalin 150 mg twice daily for diabetic neuropathy. She is on postoperative night 1 after superficial parotidectomy, and her home pregabalin was continued at the full dose along with IV hydromorphone as needed. She has received four doses overnight. She is now drowsy but arousable, with a respiratory rate of 11 and oxygen saturation of 92% on room air. Which adjustment to her pain regimen is most appropriate?",
  "choices": ["Switch the hydromorphone to IV morphine, which is shorter acting",
              "Add IV ketorolac every 6 hours so the opioid can be reduced",
              "Add diphenhydramine at night to improve her sleep and comfort",
              "Cut the opioid and renally dose the pregabalin"],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": "Gabapentinoids add to the respiratory depression caused by opioids, and the FDA has warned about this combination, particularly in older adults, patients with lung disease, and those with impaired kidney function. Pregabalin is cleared by the kidneys, so her full home dose accumulates with an eGFR of 32. The deciding clues are her age, kidney disease, full-dose pregabalin, and repeated opioid doses leading to sedation and slowed breathing. The best adjustment is to reduce or stop the opioid, dose the pregabalin for her kidney function, use acetaminophen as a base, and monitor her closely with continuous pulse oximetry. Naloxone should be ready if she becomes harder to arouse. Morphine is a poor choice in kidney disease because its active metabolites accumulate.",
  "why_wrong": ["Morphine's active metabolites accumulate in kidney disease and can deepen sedation and respiratory depression.",
                "Ketorolac and other NSAIDs are best avoided in significant chronic kidney disease.",
                "Diphenhydramine adds sedation and anticholinergic effects and raises the risk of delirium in older adults.",
                "Correct."],
  "board_pearl": "Gabapentinoids plus opioids cause additive respiratory depression, especially with kidney disease; renally dose the gabapentinoid and cut the opioid.",
  "curveball": "An hour later she is difficult to arouse and her respiratory rate is 6. What do you do?",
  "curveball_answer": "Support her airway and breathing, call for help, and give naloxone in small, repeated IV doses to restore breathing without causing abrupt severe pain. Because hydromorphone can outlast naloxone, she needs continued monitoring and possibly a naloxone infusion. Review all sedating drugs and transfer her to a monitored setting if needed.",
  "learning_stage": "mastery",
  "focus": "pharmacology"},
