 {"topic": "Geriatric Otolaryngology / Frailty",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-geriatric-otolaryngology-frailty",
  "stem": "A 79-year-old widower who lives alone reports that over several years his sense of smell has faded so that he can no longer smell food or flowers. He has no nasal obstruction, rhinorrhea, head injury, or recent viral illness, and his neurologic examination is normal. Nasal endoscopy is normal. Standardized smell testing shows severe hyposmia. He cooks on a gas stove. Which counseling point is most important for him now?",
  "choices": ["A 2-week course of oral prednisone is likely to restore his sense of smell",
              "He should have working smoke and natural gas detectors and check food dates rather than relying on smell",
              "Daily zinc supplementation is the evidence-based treatment for age-related smell loss",
              "His smell loss is a normal part of aging and has no safety or health implications"],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": "Smell loss is common in older adults and is often gradual, bilateral, and related to age-related changes in the olfactory system. It poses real safety risks, because affected people cannot detect smoke, gas leaks, or spoiled food. The deciding clues are that he lives alone, cooks with gas, and has severe hyposmia. Counseling should focus on smoke and natural gas detectors, labeling and dating food, and asking others to check for odors. Olfactory training is a low-risk option that may help some patients. Smell loss in older adults is also associated with nutritional problems, depression, neurodegenerative disease, and higher mortality, so it should not be dismissed.",
  "why_wrong": ["Oral steroids help some inflammatory causes of smell loss, but not gradual age-related loss with a normal endoscopy.",
                "Correct.",
                "Zinc has not been shown to improve age-related smell loss and is not a recommended treatment.",
                "Smell loss in older adults carries real safety, nutritional, and health implications."],
  "board_pearl": "Older adults with smell loss need safety counseling: smoke and gas detectors and food dating.",
  "curveball": "He asks whether anything can actually improve his sense of smell. What can you offer?",
  "curveball_answer": "Olfactory training, which involves smelling a set of strong odors twice daily for several months, is a low-cost option with some evidence of benefit in various types of smell loss. Results are modest, especially in age-related loss, so expectations should be realistic. Treatable contributors such as medications or nasal disease should be addressed if present.",
  "learning_stage": "application",
  "focus": "counseling"},
