 {"topic": "Immunocompromised Host in Otolaryngology",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-immunocompromised-host-in-otolaryngology",
  "stem": "A 52-year-old man with acute myeloid leukemia has been neutropenic for 3 weeks and has been on voriconazole prophylaxis throughout. He develops fever, right facial pain, and a black eschar on the right middle turbinate and palate. Frozen section of debrided tissue shows broad, ribbon-like hyphae with few septations and wide-angle branching invading blood vessels. Surgical debridement is under way. Which antifungal change is most appropriate?",
  "choices": ["Continue voriconazole at a higher dose",
              "Switch to micafungin",
              "Switch to fluconazole",
              "Start a lipid formulation of amphotericin B"],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": "Broad, ribbon-like, pauciseptate hyphae with wide-angle branching and vascular invasion are typical of Mucorales, the cause of mucormycosis. Voriconazole has no reliable activity against Mucorales, and voriconazole exposure is a recognized risk factor for breakthrough mucormycosis in patients with hematologic cancers. The deciding clue is that this infection developed while he was taking voriconazole. A lipid formulation of amphotericin B is the first-line treatment, with isavuconazole or posaconazole as alternatives or step-down options. Treatment also requires aggressive surgical debridement and reversing immune suppression where possible, such as with growth factors. Echinocandins and fluconazole have no useful activity against Mucorales.",
  "why_wrong": ["Voriconazole lacks activity against Mucorales regardless of dose, and this infection broke through it.",
                "Echinocandins such as micafungin have no useful activity against Mucorales.",
                "Fluconazole has no activity against Mucorales or other molds.",
                "Correct."],
  "board_pearl": "Invasive fungal sinusitis breaking through voriconazole suggests mucormycosis; switch to lipid amphotericin B.",
  "curveball": "If the frozen section had instead shown narrow, regularly septate hyphae with acute-angle branching, how would treatment differ?",
  "curveball_answer": "Narrow septate hyphae with acute-angle branching suggest Aspergillus, for which voriconazole or isavuconazole is first-line therapy. Breakthrough on voriconazole would prompt checking drug levels and resistance testing, and possibly a change in class. Surgical debridement and immune recovery remain essential in either case.",
  "learning_stage": "mastery",
  "focus": "pharmacology"},
