 {"topic": "Oral Manifestations of Systemic Disease",
  "concept_id": "v6-general-ent-emergencies-oral-manifestations-of-systemic-disease",
  "stem": "A 16-year-old boy is referred for cosmetic concerns about dark spots on his lips. Examination shows multiple small, flat, brown-black macules clustered on the lips, crossing the vermilion border, on the buccal mucosa, and on his fingertips. He has had two episodes of severe colicky abdominal pain in the past year, one requiring an emergency visit, and he has mild iron-deficiency anemia. His father died of pancreatic cancer at age 52. What is the most appropriate next step?",
  "choices": ["Laser removal of the lip macules for cosmetic improvement",
              "Referral for gastrointestinal endoscopic evaluation and genetic testing for STK11",
              "An ACTH stimulation test to look for adrenal insufficiency",
              "Reassurance that these are benign freckles that need no further evaluation"],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": "Clustered dark macules on the lips, buccal mucosa, and fingers, together with colicky abdominal pain, anemia, and a family history of early pancreatic cancer, point to Peutz-Jeghers syndrome. This autosomal dominant condition is usually caused by STK11 mutations and causes hamartomatous gastrointestinal polyps that can bleed or lead to intussusception. The deciding clues are mucosal pigment crossing onto the buccal mucosa, which is unusual for ordinary freckles, and the abdominal history. Patients have a markedly increased lifetime risk of gastrointestinal, pancreatic, breast, and other cancers. They need referral for endoscopic evaluation, genetic testing and counseling, and lifelong cancer surveillance. The macules themselves are benign and can be treated cosmetically later if desired.",
  "why_wrong": ["Laser treatment addresses appearance only and would miss a syndrome with serious cancer and intussusception risk.",
                "Correct.",
                "Adrenal insufficiency causes diffuse pigmentation of the skin and gums and would not explain the polyp symptoms or family history.",
                "Ordinary freckles do not involve the buccal mucosa, and his abdominal pain and family history need evaluation."],
  "board_pearl": "Perioral and buccal pigmented macules with colicky abdominal pain suggest Peutz-Jeghers syndrome: refer for GI evaluation and STK11 testing.",
  "curveball": "A different patient has diffuse brown pigmentation of the gums and buccal mucosa, fatigue, weight loss, and low blood pressure. What is the likely cause?",
  "curveball_answer": "Diffuse mucosal and skin hyperpigmentation with fatigue, weight loss, and hypotension suggests primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease). High ACTH levels stimulate melanocytes, causing the pigmentation. Morning cortisol and ACTH levels, followed by an ACTH stimulation test, confirm the diagnosis.",
  "learning_stage": "foundation",
  "focus": "workup"},
